        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "357a87d9-d33f-3cbc-92a8-91ec0fa185fa",
                "word": "bitcoin",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "efb332eb-7dc4-4961-a3da-8f4dca82c099",
                        "entry_id": "357a87d9-d33f-3cbc-92a8-91ec0fa185fa",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "bitcoins"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "bfdc31fe-16bd-3dfa-907e-b2e7d7d61290",
                        "entry_id": "357a87d9-d33f-3cbc-92a8-91ec0fa185fa",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Monnaie numérique d'échange chiffrée et décentralisée. Son symbole est ₿ (Unicode U+20BF). Son code d’échange international est BTC ou XBT."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0aa223fa-419f-4d15-a5b1-d46e0b473e04",
                                "sense_id": "bfdc31fe-16bd-3dfa-907e-b2e7d7d61290",
                                "text": "Gardez à l’esprit que le Bitcoin repose sur un marché non régulé, cette monnaie virtuelle n’a pas de cours officiel."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "667d9c54-3a6d-4db8-9a91-b34d4f8bcddd",
                                "sense_id": "bfdc31fe-16bd-3dfa-907e-b2e7d7d61290",
                                "text": "Le Bitcoin est une valeur erratique et spéculative."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "de4f0a8e-7df0-4aa5-bc9f-602c480852db",
                                "sense_id": "bfdc31fe-16bd-3dfa-907e-b2e7d7d61290",
                                "text": "\"Que le bitcoin vaille 1 dollar ou 1 million de dollars n’a donc d’importance que pour les spéculateurs qui en détiennent, qui voient leur patrimoine augmenter ou fondre en fonction des fluctuations du cours\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "60c388b6-e493-472a-9781-b39a6cc1caad",
                                "sense_id": "bfdc31fe-16bd-3dfa-907e-b2e7d7d61290",
                                "text": "Timbres-poste, pièces rares, internet, casinos, bitcoins, mandats télégraphiques et l’on a vu dans l’actualité ce que valaient les fouilles dans les aéroports, sont des véhicules de paiement discrets."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "87b136db-a7a2-406d-9c92-8b84c30167e1",
                                "sense_id": "bfdc31fe-16bd-3dfa-907e-b2e7d7d61290",
                                "text": "Le bitcoin est défini comme une donnée informatique qui passe directement de l’ordinateur du vendeur à celui de l’acheteur en P2P (peer to peer, d’ordinateur à ordinateur [, poste à poste, pair à pair]). Ces monnaies virtuelles représentent donc l’outil rêvé pour les transactions qui nécessitent une opacité extrême et une discrétion totale comme celles qui participent au blanchiment de l’argent sale."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2fc3c6c8-8946-4ec6-a1fa-a1a9f028df26",
                                "sense_id": "bfdc31fe-16bd-3dfa-907e-b2e7d7d61290",
                                "text": "Il se fera payer et achètera ses armes en bitcoins, cette cryptomonnaie aux mouvements intraçables."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b7b8ad8b-8746-4f4e-8a8a-be946a8c4d03",
                                "sense_id": "bfdc31fe-16bd-3dfa-907e-b2e7d7d61290",
                                "text": "Seulement pour le bitcoin, les centres de cryptominage nécessiteraient une consommation annuelle de 121,56 térawattheures dans le monde, selon un outil développé par le Cambridge Centre for Alternative Finance (CCAF). C’est davantage que la consommation de l’Argentine ou de la Norvège."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8acd52cd-781c-456f-ac14-49590b8ad0c8",
                                "sense_id": "bfdc31fe-16bd-3dfa-907e-b2e7d7d61290",
                                "text": "Une étude a révélé que moins de 10 000 personnes dans le monde possèdent collectivement 4,8 millions de bitcoins (12), soit près d’un tiers des 18,5 millions de bitcoins extraits à ce jour. Ces derniers représentent une valeur de marché de près de 600 milliards d’USD. La situation n’a guère changé. L’écosystème du bitcoin se concentre autour de quelques grands acteurs qui continuent de le dominer, qu’il s’agisse de mineurs (13), de détenteurs de bitcoin ou de changeurs. Cette concentration expose le bitcoin au risque systémique et implique également que la majorité des gains tirés des nouvelles adoptions sont susceptibles de revenir de manière disproportionnée à un nombre restreint de participants (14)."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "432be0c3-2848-35b8-8290-32e09e50ea31",
                        "entry_id": "357a87d9-d33f-3cbc-92a8-91ec0fa185fa",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Une de ses unités"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c61974f0-8aa1-44cf-aca4-d75ecbe6f02d",
                                "sense_id": "432be0c3-2848-35b8-8290-32e09e50ea31",
                                "text": "le coût de production d’un seul BTC est désormais de 53 000 dollars, contre 29 500 dollars au dernier trimestre de 2023."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8679b6b1-f057-4ef4-bc1d-2c10cad8af58",
                        "source_entry_id": "357a87d9-d33f-3cbc-92a8-91ec0fa185fa",
                        "target_lang_word": "bitcoin",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "139f6a17-10ce-43c0-a7e1-6ff532474891",
                        "source_entry_id": "357a87d9-d33f-3cbc-92a8-91ec0fa185fa",
                        "target_lang_word": "bitcoin",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "bitcoin",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a874e2e3-6590-3098-8ee8-08320ce9396a",
                                        "entry_id": "8f71a53f-4f87-34da-95bc-a023f94af5e7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A decentralized cryptocurrency using blockchain technology."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d1c70aeb-5d63-40b0-9a76-990da8f794ad",
                                                "sense_id": "a874e2e3-6590-3098-8ee8-08320ce9396a",
                                                "text": "We do not accept bitcoin at this store."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a314f8f8-9d61-400a-b8cd-c7bc1493d3d0",
                                                "sense_id": "a874e2e3-6590-3098-8ee8-08320ce9396a",
                                                "text": "Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System [title] […] What is needed is an electronic payment system based on cryptographic proof instead of trust, allowing any two willing parties to transact directly with each other without the need for a trusted third party. […] In this paper, we propose a solution to the double-spending problem using a peer-to-peer distributed timestamp server to generate computational proof of the chronological order of transactions."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cdbb4c65-b80e-42c0-b59e-f6e153b6c01d",
                                                "sense_id": "a874e2e3-6590-3098-8ee8-08320ce9396a",
                                                "text": "[A] man named Satoshi Nakamoto posted a research paper to an obscure cryptography listserv describing his design for a new digital currency that he called bitcoin."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "65b1cc9b-853b-455a-88d9-eb7fb2555499",
                                                "sense_id": "a874e2e3-6590-3098-8ee8-08320ce9396a",
                                                "text": "[Bitcoins] do not depend on the state banks, but are created through mining. Mining is also called as hashing. It is a process of solving complex math problems using those computers which run bitcoin software."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d03daee5-3e28-4213-a780-34ebb33bd628",
                                                "sense_id": "a874e2e3-6590-3098-8ee8-08320ce9396a",
                                                "text": "Other e-commerce sites that accept bitcoin are: Bitcoinshop.us which offers products like watches and air conditioners and ships to people within the United States; […] Travel booking websites also start to accept bitcoin sales on flights, hotel bookings to at least 200,000 hotels and railway Amtrak through their platforms."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2e3d4708-384b-4fff-a0f5-188eb8318f08",
                                                "sense_id": "a874e2e3-6590-3098-8ee8-08320ce9396a",
                                                "text": "Individual bitcoin users are not the only driving force in terms of securing the bitcoin network. Over the past few years, dedicated bitcoin nodes have been added to the network with the sole purpose of receiving and broadcasting new bitcoin transactions to other users and nodes on the network."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "892a9f43-e355-40d5-99d4-438320f38f9e",
                                                "sense_id": "a874e2e3-6590-3098-8ee8-08320ce9396a",
                                                "text": "Find a friend who has bitcoin and buy some from him or her directly. Many bitcoin users start this way. This method is the least complicated. One way to meet people with bitcoin is to attend a local bitcoin meetup listed at Meetup.com (https://meetup.com)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "bitcoin",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1390a58e-eec4-35df-856a-6dc106be962e",
                                        "entry_id": "28277a83-91cd-3c63-a938-60ef3ac73889",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A unit of the bitcoin (proper noun sense 1) cryptocurrency."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "963c15a7-6322-498f-ba19-08422ff07079",
                                                "sense_id": "1390a58e-eec4-35df-856a-6dc106be962e",
                                                "text": "This item costs one bitcoin."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "da7d70df-8ed8-4a55-b91a-babf462c4dcf",
                                                "sense_id": "1390a58e-eec4-35df-856a-6dc106be962e",
                                                "text": "The value of fiat money is determined by legal means (legal tender laws). In other words, fiat money is anything the government says in money. For instance, if the government says an IOU or a bitcoin is money, then it is money. The major currencies of the contemporary world are fiat money."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "54c808f8-c72c-477e-8f85-69e5e7defec3",
                                                "sense_id": "1390a58e-eec4-35df-856a-6dc106be962e",
                                                "text": "Users willing to devote CPU power to running a special piece of software would be called miners and would form a network to maintain the block chain collectively. […] The first miner to solve each puzzle would be awarded 50 new bitcoins, and the associated block of transactions would be added to the chain. […] [Satoshi] Nakamoto himself mined the first 50 bitcoins—which came to be called the genesis block—on January 3, 2009."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "efa454b5-c4e2-40e5-bc51-faf644b07b73",
                                                "sense_id": "1390a58e-eec4-35df-856a-6dc106be962e",
                                                "text": "Each time you succeed in creating a hash, you are rewarded with 25 bitcoins, the block chain is updated and all the users on the network are notified. This serves as an incentive to keep mining the bitcoins and keep the bitcoin transactions working."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "821bf3fd-f964-467d-a46a-d74bc2c5f302",
                                                "sense_id": "1390a58e-eec4-35df-856a-6dc106be962e",
                                                "text": "They buy bitcoins at a lower price then sell them at a higher value."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2841c37e-f4c1-42e1-a357-3877ba8968dc",
                                                "sense_id": "1390a58e-eec4-35df-856a-6dc106be962e",
                                                "text": "But consider that a significant amount of bitcoin is also being held by investors. Those investors do not plan to sell their bitcoin for some time because they are speculating that due to its utility as MoIP, demand will continue to rise and so too will its value. Currently, roughly 5.5 million bitcoin, or US$5.5 billion worth at the price of US$1,000 per coin, is held by the top 1,000 addresses recorded in Bitcoin's blockchain."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6ae474bf-28cd-4015-b4d2-094dc8d9059c",
                                                "sense_id": "1390a58e-eec4-35df-856a-6dc106be962e",
                                                "text": "Bitcoin is the oldest and best known of all crypto coins. Its creator — the ever-anonymous Satoshi Nakamoto — set things up so that there can never be more than 21 million bitcoins in existence.\nUnlike other assets that are valued based on tangible components — for example, a company’s goods and services or a natural resource — bitcoin is considered a store of value, the price of which depends strictly on what others are willing to pay for it."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
